edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499377 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7a04f70f-7747-529e-a1eb-8502c8e8a0b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499394 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499394 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499394 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499394 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499394 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499394 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499394 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499394 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499394 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "aad6a91e-f93b-5ecf-b16f-9fda253dd13c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499406 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499406 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499406 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499406 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499406 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c892b43d-4540-59a6-b73b-33d123922a45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499413 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499413 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499413 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499413 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499413 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d56a4bca-54d6-5320-a849-6ac02a4b2246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499452 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c1/c13c5ac52b7bc508032d2d53b1169730641bafefcd8c0f5487d96699d6120946.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $P$ and $Q$ shown above. Given that $WXY≅FGH,$ what is the perimeter of $Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25b1bc91-494d-53b8-abed-15f163bc2377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499452 #edb/ref 1074 "The statement $WXY≅FGH$ means that $P$ and $Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $W$ corresponds to vertex $F,$\n\n- vertex $X$ corresponds to vertex $G,$\n\n- vertex $Y$ corresponds to vertex $H.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}FG & =WX \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}HF & =YW \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe figure shows that $FG=GH.$ Therefore, the perimeter of $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{Q} & =FG+GH+HF \\\\  & =5+5+5\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =5(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499465 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499465 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499465 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499465 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9a/9a4182f96ede84fdae27964aa21fc0537a37e279ec7eb7c390aeb94d1cae8eb9.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the pentagons shown in the diagram above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "486306f3-8fb2-5f6e-b7bd-26ff06793aaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499465 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $∠W≅∠Z.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second pentagon that correspond to $Z.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}YZ & =MN \\\\ ZV & =NJ \\\\ ∠Z & ≅∠N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $Z$ corresponds to vertex $N,$\n\n- consequently, vertex $W$ corresponds to vertex $K.$\n\nAs for the other three vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠X & ≅∠L \\\\ ∠Y & ≅∠M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =ML \\\\ WX & =LK.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $X$ corresponds to vertex $L,$\n\n- vertex $Y$ corresponds to vertex $M,$ and\n\n- vertex $V$ corresponds to vertex $J$ because they both correspond to the right angle.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nVWXYZ≅JKLMN.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499471 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499471 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499471 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d4/d46182f3d4373a6b8bb1d330afb51a4ed2eea0c33b2f929b5538d7e0edf76353.png)\n\nGiven that $ABCDE≅MPQRT,$ find $x-2y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56adf5b0-549f-5613-9c35-0245d2557d7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499471 #edb/ref 1074 "The statement $ABCDE≅MPQRT$ means that $P$ and $Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $M,$\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to vertex $P,$\n\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $Q,$\n\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to vertex $R,$\n\n- vertex $E$ corresponds to vertex $T.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MP & =AB \\\\ y-4 & =2 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠E & =m∠T \\\\ {(3x+20)}^{∘} & ={140}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+20 & =140 \\\\ 3x & =120 \\\\ x & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx-2y=40-12=28.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499475 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499475 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499475 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499475 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a0/a09bde30c164f4ca5bf964690e734057efe0344f1136c611d04492a85cc8d4bb.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499475 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a54090b-41ad-5e7f-a026-590e30e34f07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499475 #edb/ref 1074 "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is a triangle (it has $3$ sides). So, the polygons $R$ and $S$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $3$ sides.\n\n- The polygons $U$ and  $Q$ are *not* congruent to $P$ (even though they both have $3$ sides) because $U$ and $Q$ don't have the same shape with $P.$\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $T$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
